  "markdown_report": "The best next step at 10% progress remains an **anchor-preserving HSTU context-access audit**, strengthened with implementation checks and a positional-bias factorial design. No measured improvement or validated sensitivity-to-training-benefit relationship is available.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Compacted deletion changes evidence and geometry together; preserve slots to separate them. (2) Latest-item removal changes the readout anchor; retain it as a separate condition. (3) Training-target probes measure fitted-model sensitivity; bootstrap intervals cannot establish generalization.\n\n**Insights from related work.** (1) HSTU uses pointwise SiLU attention, relative bias and multiplicative gating [1,2]. Blocking must follow these semantics. (2) RecPO supports recency in its particular objective, but rating-based intensity is unavailable here [3]. Compatibility requires explicit evidence, with unknown states when attributes are missing [4]. (3) Sampled ranking and ties can affect comparisons [5,6]; diagnostics cannot replace host selection. RNN recency-dropout results motivate experiments without validating transfer to HSTU [7].\n\n**Framework and directions.** Cross intervention mechanism (context access, position bias, separate anchor removal), evidence source (training sensitivity, host validation, exploratory feedback), and claim strength (observed response, proxy association, unsupported intent). This organizes architectural sensitivity, temporal relevance and evaluation reliability, exposing the gap between dependence and beneficial regularization.\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality/potential/code difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means more effort.\n\n- **Matched factorial context-access audit [1,2]: 5/7/6.** Originality: focused HSTU adaptation; established ablation principles. Potential: identifies mechanisms; sensitivity may not predict gains. Difficulty: reusable reporting; verified attention hooks require care.\n- **Anchor-preserving context dropout [7]: 3/6/4.** Originality: targeted transfer; recency dropout already exists. Potential: may reduce brittle dependence; can erase useful evidence. Difficulty: modest training change; exact masking needs verification.\n- **Evidence-qualified recommendation audit [4]: 4/6/3.** Originality: explicit unknown states; established metadata auditing. Potential: clarifies unsupported claims; sparse descriptions limit coverage. Difficulty: lightweight rules; ambiguous attributes require review.\n\n**Selected algorithm.** Freeze each category’s single HSTU. Keep eligible training users, targets and 99 unique unseen negatives fixed. Match recent and older blocked-key counts, excluding the final observed item. Apply gates after SiLU at every layer/head, preserving slots, timestamps, padded width, residuals and readout. Cross intact/recent/older blocking with position bias present/removed, holding time bias fixed [8]. Report all six cells, conditional effects and their interaction; probe time bias separately when supported. Include count-matched random controls and distance distributions; causal eligibility differs across intermediate queries.\n\nVerify empty-gate equivalence, blocked-occurrence perturbation/Jacobian isolation, unblocked sensitivity and future-token invariance. Report paired HR@10, target rank, margins, overlap, ties, exclusions and missingness. Bootstrap users within category using shared indices across cells; equally average four category effects [9]. Intervals condition on checkpoints and candidates. Prespecify hypotheses; use unchanged host validation for subsequent training selection. Preserve diagnostic interfaces, routing, one checkpoint/category, seeds 0/42, supplied splits, 200 maximum epochs and 200 batches/epoch.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Audit each category’s single trained HSTU using matched recent-versus-older key-access interventions that preserve sequence slots and the final observed readout item. Cross these interventions with position-bias removal while holding temporal bias fixed. Keep anchor removal and temporal-bias removal as separate probes, and distinguish inference sensitivity from evidence about retrained models.",
    "motivation": "Existing compacted deletion probes confound evidence removal with positional changes. Qualitative feedback motivates investigating recent specific connections, but cannot establish current purchase purpose, compatibility, or an expected benefit from recency regularization. At 10% progress, a verified audit is more defensible than selecting a new training mechanism from unvalidated sensitivity estimates. Originality and potential are therefore revised downward, and implementation difficulty upward, to reflect established ablation methods, uncertain predictive validity, and HSTU-specific masking requirements.",
    "implementation_notes": "Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), metrics and metric_definitions. Preserve the actual trained view.model, item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), user_train, meta_data and review_data contracts. Do not use unavailable review content, ratings, categories, prices, stores or simulator traits.\n\nInspect the supplied corrected HSTU implementation before adding optional hooks; the public reference is guidance rather than proof that the host backbone is identical. In the referenced formulation, A = SiLU(QK^T + position_bias + time_bias) / N multiplied by the causal allow-mask. Apply a broadcast per-example key-column gate valid[b,j] * (1-blocked[b,j]) after SiLU at every layer and head. Preserve the original causal diagonal, query validity, padded attention width N, position offsets, timestamps, residual update, normalization and dropout semantics. Never compact, renormalize over surviving keys, or assume that zeroing K removes bias-driven attention. Leave default forward behavior unchanged. If position and temporal terms cannot be separated, report the factorial component as unsupported rather than interpreting removal of both as a position-only intervention.\n\nUse the greatest nonzero sequence index as readout, never count-1. Reserve the final training item as diagnostic target and left-pad its preceding history using the baseline maximum length. Exclude the final observed item from all blocking because its residual, query and gate paths remain active. Define context as the remaining retained nonpadding positions. Require at least two context positions; choose k=min(5,floor(context_count/2)), block the k most recent versus k oldest context positions, and record intervening positions. Include a uniformly sampled k-position control using a dedicated local RNG seeded 42. Final-query blocked counts match; intermediate queries can have different numbers of causally eligible blocked keys. Report those counts and distance distributions rather than claiming per-query cardinality matching or content effects independent of distance. No-backfill compacted deletions and last-ten suffixes may remain separately labeled legacy probes; they do not identify geometry-preserving effects. Final-item removal is also separate because it changes the anchor.\n\nUse at most 200 eligible training users/category, chosen reproducibly with a local seed-42 RNG, recording actual counts and exclusion reasons. Use one fixed panel per user containing the target and 99 unique uniform negatives from valid catalog IDs absent from that user’s entire training history. Exclude users with insufficient negatives. Cache candidate IDs, their order, sampling definitions and user IDs beneath the trial directory. These diagnostic panels are distinct from the independently administered host validation panels. Never change the pinned host evaluator, its 200 users/category, negative sampling, ordering or tie policy. Document the host policy when supplied. For auxiliary probes, use a fixed seeded candidate permutation and stable descending-score ranking, with the same order in every cell; report positive cutoff-tie frequency and nonfinite scores.\n\nRun the six factorial cells: blocking in {none,recent,older} crossed with position bias in {present,removed}, with time bias unchanged. Record per-user target rank, HR@10, target-minus-highest-negative margin, top-10 overlap with the corresponding intact cell and paired changes. Define the interaction as (HR_recent,on-HR_older,on)-(HR_recent,off-HR_older,off). Also report recent-versus-intact and older-versus-intact effects conditional on position-bias state. Time-bias removal, when supported, is a separately labeled analysis. These are inference interventions on one checkpoint, not retrained architectural ablations.\n\nBefore interpreting results, require empty intervention agreement with original features and candidate scores, using dtype-appropriate tolerances recorded in the artifact. Perturb only blocked embedding occurrences after lookup, holding candidate embeddings fixed even when tables are tied; readout features and scores should remain unchanged within tolerance. In a separate gradient-enabled evaluation pass, require a zero readout Jacobian with respect to blocked occurrences; use unblocked perturbations as a sensitivity control. Test future-token invariance at earlier readouts, causal diagonal handling, and correct final-nonpadding readout under several left-padding lengths. Do not demand score invariance across changed padded widths: N and positional semantics can legitimately change outputs. Run ordinary probes under no_grad in evaluation mode, restore module modes and Python/NumPy/Torch RNG states, and keep correctness-test gradients isolated from training.\n\nBootstrap users independently within each category with 2,000 percentile resamples and a dedicated seed-42 RNG. Reuse each category’s resampled indices across every condition, then recompute category means, equally weighted four-category macro means, paired differences and interactions. Report 95% intervals, sample counts and bootstrap settings. Repeated panels or conditions are not independent users. Intervals condition on the fitted checkpoints and chosen candidates, excluding training-seed and negative-sampling uncertainty. Do not present a four-category macro estimate when category reports are missing.\n\nPrespecify the sensitivity contrast and its direction before inspecting results. Large recent-blocking damage indicates dependence on recent context, which may be useful; it does not identify harmful overreliance. Targets previously used in optimization make this an in-sample audit. Establishing predictive credibility requires a prospective prediction on disjoint allowed diagnostic discovery/confirmation cohorts or independent permitted data; repeatedly selecting on the same validation users remains exploratory. Any diagnostic holdout must be explicitly auxiliary and cannot alter the supplied benchmark splits.\n\nIf budget permits a follow-up training study, compare matched baseline, dropout-only, position-bias-regularization-only and combined HSTU configurations sequentially, each with one independently trained model/category and seed 42. Specify dropout rate and the bias penalty before training; do not choose them retrospectively to explain audit findings. These are alternative trial configurations, never fused predictors. Select only by unchanged host macro validation HR@10, report category outcomes, and reserve hidden test evaluation for the final selected configuration. No expected improvement is asserted. Retain preprocessing seed 0, maximum 200 epochs, 200 batches/epoch, single-GPU execution and the shared 43,200 GPU-second search budget; audit and debugging costs count too.\n\nRetain embedding-cosine diagnostics on up to 2,048 valid nonzero item embeddings and sampled positive-negative margins from at most ten loader batches, with explicit score-scale limitations. Use only supplied histories, titles, descriptions and available timestamps for qualitative audits. Attach evidence to compatibility/near-duplicate flags, preserve unknown states and coverage rates, and treat twenty-user feedback as exploratory. Preserve DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local artifacts and all four category routes. Any deployed architecture change must update the predictor writer and load exactly one checkpoint/category. No executable baseline replacement is specified because the actual backbone source was not supplied.",
    "pseudocode": "prespecify_context_sets_metrics_hypotheses_and_seeds()\nfor category in four_categories:\n    model = category_single_checkpoint.eval()\n    verify_default_and_empty_intervention_agreement(model)\n    verify_blocked_occurrence_isolation_with_fixed_candidate_embeddings(model)\n    verify_causality_diagonal_and_left_padding_readout(model)\n    records = []\n    for history in seeded_eligible_training_users(limit=200):\n        target = history[-1]\n        sequence, timestamps = baseline_left_pad_and_truncate(history[:-1])\n        anchor = greatest_nonzero_index(sequence)\n        context = valid_positions(sequence) excluding anchor\n        if len(context) < 2: record_exclusion(); continue\n        k = min(5, len(context) // 2)\n        recent, older = context[-k:], context[:k]\n        random_control = local_rng_sample(context, k)\n        candidates = cached_target_and_99_unique_uniform_training_unseen_negatives()\n        if candidates_unavailable: record_exclusion(); continue\n        for position_bias in [present, removed]:\n            if component_not_separable: record_unsupported(); continue\n            for blocked in [empty, recent, older, random_control]:\n                features = forward_with_post_silu_key_gates(\n                    sequence, timestamps, blocked,\n                    every_layer=True, every_head=True,\n                    position_bias=position_bias, time_bias=unchanged,\n                    preserve_slots_width_residuals=True)\n                scores = predict_score(features[:, anchor], candidates)\n                record_rank_hit_margin_overlap_ties_and_eligible_key_counts(scores)\n        separately_record_supported_time_bias_and_legacy_anchor_probes()\n    save_records_settings_missingness_and_metric_definitions(records)\nbootstrap_users_within_category(shared_cell_indices=True, resamples=2000, seed=42)\nreport_all_cells_conditional_effects_and_interaction_with_95_percent_intervals()\ncompute_equal_category_macro_only_if_all_four_reports_exist()\n# Training-target sensitivity is in-sample; it does not establish beneficial regularization.\n# Select any subsequent single-HSTU training configuration using unchanged host macro HR@10.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Combines HSTU-correct key blocking, anchor preservation and a position-bias factorial contrast into a focused, reproducible diagnostic adaptation.",
      "negative": "Ablation, factorial contrasts and paired uncertainty estimation are established methods; no new learning algorithm or demonstrated sensitivity-to-benefit relationship is provided."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "A verified audit can identify masking errors and distinguish context-access dependence from position-bias interactions, supporting more disciplined single-model experiments.",
      "negative": "Inference perturbations may be out of distribution, and training-target sensitivity may neither generalize nor predict which retrained configuration improves host validation."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "Reuses existing histories, scoring interfaces, candidate caching and reporting without fitting additional diagnostic models.",
      "negative": "Requires access to actual HSTU internals, separable bias controls, occurrence-level gradient tests and careful handling of causal eligibility, tied embeddings and numerical tolerances."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "[1] Actions Speak Louder than Words: Trillion-Parameter Sequential Transducers for Generative Recommendations",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17152",
      "contributions": [
        "Introduces HSTU with pointwise SiLU attention, relative attention bias and multiplicative feature interaction.",
        "Provides architectural motivation for separating context access, positional bias and temporal bias."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish the proposed matched-context factorial diagnostic.",
        "Does not demonstrate that frozen-model sensitivity predicts regularization gains on the supplied Amazon benchmark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "[2] HSTU public research implementation",
      "link": "https://github.com/meta-recsys/generative-recommenders/blob/main/generative_recommenders/research/modeling/sequential/hstu.py",
      "contributions": [
        "Documents reference attention semantics, including post-SiLU allow-masking, padded-width scaling and relative-bias components.",
        "Supports implementing blocked key contributions without compacting histories or renormalizing attention."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The supplied corrected host backbone may differ from this implementation.",
        "A moving repository URL is insufficient for exact reproduction; record the actual implementation version and intervention code used."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "[3] RecPO",
      "link": "https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.656.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "The supplied search evidence describes preference-intensity and recency-margin ablations in LLM preference optimization.",
        "The supplied summary reports that adding recency improves four of five evaluated datasets, illustrating context-dependent benefits."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Rating-based preference intensity uses information unavailable in this task.",
        "Its objective and model setting do not establish a universal recency mechanism or transfer to HSTU."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "[4] ECCR",
      "link": "https://genai-personalization.github.io/assets/papers/GenAIRecP2024/Li.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Distinguishes compatibility from broad relevance and co-purchase association.",
        "Motivates evidence-qualified compatibility audits with explicit unknown states."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Specific compatibility may depend on attributes absent from available titles and descriptions.",
        "Explanation plausibility and metadata flags do not establish improved ranking accuracy or actual purchase purpose."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "[5] On Sampled Metrics for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/",
      "contributions": [
        "Shows that sampled ranking metrics can disagree with full-catalog model comparisons."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not justify changing the mandated host evaluator.",
        "Repeated negative panels quantify sampling variability without eliminating sampled-metric bias."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "[6] Computing Information Retrieval Performance Measures Efficiently in the Presence of Tied Scores",
      "link": "https://marc.najork.org/papers/ecir2008.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Addresses evaluation in the presence of tied scores.",
        "Motivates documenting candidate order, tie policy and cutoff-tie frequency."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Tie-aware reporting does not resolve sampled-ranking bias.",
        "The host evaluator's pinned tie policy must remain unchanged."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "[7] Recency Dropout for Recurrent Recommender Systems",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/html/2201.11016",
      "contributions": [
        "Reports benefits from removing recent activities during RNN training in its studied settings.",
        "Uses Jacobian analysis to examine learned dependencies."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Studies recurrent recommenders rather than the supplied HSTU backbone.",
        "Does not validate frozen context-ablation sensitivity as a predictor of training improvements."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "[8] NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook: Full Factorial Designs",
      "link": "https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section6/pri615.htm",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides general guidance for factorial designs and interaction analysis.",
        "Supports reporting conditional blocking effects rather than assuming independent component contributions."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Provides experimental-design principles rather than HSTU-specific validation.",
        "Factorial inference interventions do not establish user-level causal effects or retrained-model performance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "[9] SciPy bootstrap documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.bootstrap.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Documents bootstrap confidence intervals and paired resampling.",
        "Supports preserving user pairing across intervention conditions."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Uncertainty depends on the resampling unit and conditioning assumptions.",
        "User bootstraps on fixed checkpoints and candidates omit training-seed and candidate-sampling uncertainty."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
